From 5a8e2fab827dc83f38cc32445b618a3d65bff0cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] application: log where the interface thread is when it makes
 no pass

"log the time without a frame in the GUI loops" says how long the
interface went without a frame, once it has one again. It does not say
what its thread was doing. The first opening of an add-on froze the
interface for 30.9 s (2026-10-03, "GUI: 30890 ms without a frame after
a nested loop; window 10025") and the log has no trace of the cause.

CGUIStallWatch is a thread that watches the time of the last pass of
the main loop and of the nested loops. After five seconds without a
pass, and once more after fifteen, it writes to the log:

- what the thread of the interface waits in: the system call, from
  /proc/self/task/<tid>/syscall, and for the calls that take one the
  file, device or socket;
- the return addresses on its stack, innermost first, as file+address
  (what addr2line takes), and by name for the functions that their
  file exports.

The watched thread is only looked at. It gets no signal: a signal ends
a poll(), a select() or a sleep with EINTR even with SA_RESTART, and
code that takes that for an error (the wait of libnfs, for one) would
fail where it only was slow. Its stack is read through the kernel
(process_vm_readv()), which cannot fault, and nothing unwinds through
code of others. Nothing is asked of the dynamic loader either, whose
records another thread can free at any moment by unloading a library,
and whose lock a thread that hangs while loading one holds: what is
mapped where comes from /proc/self/maps, and the names from the dynamic
symbol tables of the files on disk, read after the list of addresses
is in the log.

The price is exactness. The return addresses are found by their look
(a word of the stack that points into code, right behind a call
instruction), so calls that already returned can be among them. A
thread that is running, not waiting, shows no stack: the report then
says so, with the processor time it used. And a function that its file
does not export has no name; Kodi itself is linked stripped, so of it
only the exported functions have one, and the libraries of the build
resolve the rest of theirs.

Nothing happens while the loop runs or while Kodi stops (which takes
seconds by design; a report that was looking at the thread when Kodi
began to stop is not written, it would describe the thread waiting for
the watch itself), and there are at most twelve reports in an hour.
The 72 system calls it names were compared with the table of the
kernel of the image. Linux on x86-64 with the GNU C library 2.35 or
later; elsewhere the watch does nothing.
